00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:17.520
Helping nature is seen as both practical and a way to show respect for the land.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.940
Now here is something really cool about one of today's words.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.720
Want to know a secret about titsa?

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.800
The Russian word pitsa and the English word poultry are actually cousins.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.980
They both come from the same ancient Latin root pouloos,

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:37.300
which meant a young animal or chick.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.120
This word started in ancient Latin,

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.640
traveled through old French as poultry, meaning hen,

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.360
became poultry in English for farm birds,

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:57.180
and a slightly different branch came into Slavic languages as pitsa, meaning any bird.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.980
Along the way the meaning split.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:05.060
English kept the farm bird idea while Russian uses it for all birds,

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:06.580
but be careful.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.140
This creates a fun gocha with the false friend Pietok,

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:14.220
which sounds a bit like pet, but actually means rooster.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:18.320
Next time you see the English word poultry on the menu,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.360
remember it is cousins with the Russian word pitsa.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.140
That hidden family connection is your secret shortcut to remembering the word forever.

